  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe NV low will fill rapidly as it moves into the northern Rockies \nthough a trough will remain in place from near LAS southwestward \nacross SAN thence into the Pacific.  High pressure initially remains \na bit further west than previously forecast though a shift eastward \non Wednesday should allow better mid-level southwesterly flow to \noverspread the region especially along the TX/NM state line.  This \ngeneral setup will remain in place through late this week though a \nbit of a shift to more westerly directionality looks to occur by \nFriday night. High pressure will build into Texas more squarely \nover the weekend as a Pacific NW storm, initially making landfall \non Thursday, translates into the Canadian Prairies.\n\nMonday evening, we'll potentially be dealing with a weak back door \nfront.  This is most prolifically noted in the NAM but hints (albeit \nmuch weaker) do appear in the GFS/ECM guidance as well.  This could \nhelp to keep storm chances going well into the evening and possibly \nshifting south into the Permian Basin though that remains quite \nuncertain given the overarching variance in evolutionary solutions. \nTuesday and Wednesday will continue to have rain opportunities \nthough guidance generally favors ERN NM.  Still our western counties \nlook to have the best precipitation chances Wed, Thu, and Fri.  At \nthis point, Saturday looks much drier.  Storm activity this week has \nthe tendency to be predominantly late afternoon through the \novernight hours thence waning by mid morning.  Plenty of uncertainty \nexists with this batch of monsoon moisture, but as mentioned above, \nthe west looks wetter than our east. Periods of heavy rain will be\npossible.  Cooler and cloudy conditions will accompany the \nthunderstorm chances yielding more cloud cover than we've seen in \na while.",
